   275	**tier is self-declared** unless CI pins a floor (so pin `--min-tier` to the
   276	lowest tier that must always be met — the example uses L2), and branch
   277	protection's override is a human decision, not something the gate can prevent.
   278	What the gate removes is *silent* merges of unreviewed code.
   279	
   280	(If the repo vendors `bin/coverloop` — which this one does — replace the
   281	`curl` with `./bin/coverloop`, pinning the gate's version to the repo.)
   282	
   283	## Honest threat model
   284	
   285	The gate verifies that evidence **exists, passes, and is bound to the commit
   286	under review**. It cannot stop someone from hand-writing a lying report —
   287	nothing local can. What it changes is the failure mode: silent drift
   288	("the loop probably ran") becomes an explicit, committed, PR-visible
   289	artifact that a human reviewer can audit. Forging it is no longer an
   290	accident; it's a choice that leaves a trail.
   291	
   292	**What "captured" does and doesn't buy you.** `--codex-run` upgrades a verdict
   293	from a bare claim to a hashed transcript committed in the PR — forging it now
   294	means fabricating a plausible reviewer log *and* running the fake command, and
   295	the log is right there for a human to read. `verify_capture()` binds each
   296	captured verdict to *this* commit's / *this* reviewer's log
   297	(`.coverloop/reports/<commit>.<reviewer>.log`), rejects replayed, tampered, or
   298	symlinked transcripts, and checks the sha256 — so `--require-transcript` can't be
   299	satisfied by pointing at an old or off-tree file. It still does **not**
   300	cryptographically prove the real reviewer ran: a **committer** can always run a
   301	fake command that emits "CLEAN". Integrity here assumes a non-adversarial
   302	filesystem and that PR review catches an obviously bogus transcript; defeating
   303	it requires commit access and leaves a committed, readable trail. That is the
   304	honest ceiling for a tool that runs on your machine — raise it further only
   305	with a server-side/GitHub-App check (roadmap).
   306	
   307	**"Attached" is one honesty notch below that.** `--codex-log` skips even the
   308	"ran the fake command" step — the tool never executed anything, so the
   309	transcript's provenance is entirely the attester's word. Everything else holds
   310	(same redaction, same commit+reviewer binding, same tamper/replay rejection,
   311	same PR-readable log), which is why `--require-transcript` accepts it: the
   312	property that flag actually enforces is *a committed transcript a human can
   313	audit*, and both sources deliver it. The gate's `[attached]` label keeps the
   314	distinction visible instead of pretending attachment is execution. The
   315	mechanical failure modes are closed: `--*-log` refuses sources inside
   316	`.coverloop/reports/` (no one-command laundering of a withheld failed-run log
   317	or replay of an old commit's artifact), refuses withheld-placeholder content
   318	anywhere, and an entry claiming `attached` while carrying a captured run's
   319	`exit_code` is rejected as inconsistent.
   320	
   321	**Known residuals (by design, until a GitHub App exists):**
   322	- **Tier is self-declared** unless CI pins it. A PR could label an L3 change L0
   323	  to dodge gates; defense is the committed, reviewable tier field plus a pinned
   324	  floor — `coverloop gate --ci --min-tier L2` forces ≥L2 evidence on every PR
   325	  regardless of what the report claims. Pin it to your repo's risk appetite.
   326	- **Human approval is *named*, not *authenticated*.** `--approver daniel`
   327	  records who approved, but doesn't verify it was really them via a GitHub
   328	  review/environment approval. Treat the approver field as attributable intent,
   329	  and lean on branch protection's own required-reviews for identity until the
   330	  planned `--require-github-approval` lands.
   331	- **`test_command` and reviewer commands come from in-repo config/flags** — a
   332	  PR that changes them is high-signal and must be reviewed as such.
   333	- **`--require-executed` is best-effort, not proof of execution.** It prefers a
   334	  coverloop-*captured* run over an *attached* transcript, but `command`,
   335	  `exit_code`, and `ran_at` are unauthenticated JSON and the log's bytes+hash
   336	  are attester-authored, so a committer who controls the working tree can still
   337	  hand-craft a captured-shaped entry. It filters honestly-attached evidence (a
   338	  useful policy signal), it does not cryptographically prove coverloop ran the
   339	  reviewer — the same non-adversarial-filesystem ceiling as the rest of the
   340	  tool. (A genuine *failed* capture cannot be laundered, though: its log carries
   341	  a withheld-run marker the gate rejects for both sources.)
   342	- **Dirty-worktree drift is caught only with `--require-clean-tree`.** Evidence
   343	  binds to HEAD, so uncommitted code edited *after* a passing attest would gate
   344	  green while HEAD is unchanged. CI is immune (it checks out the clean PR head
   345	  SHA); for **local** pre-push/pre-deploy gating pass `--require-clean-tree`,
   346	  which fails on uncommitted non-evidence changes (report artifacts carved out).
   347	  It is opt-in because a legitimate untracked scratch file would otherwise block
   348	  the gate.
   349	
   350	`--json` emits a machine-readable verdict on stdout; `--ci` adds GitHub
   351	error annotations on **stderr** so failures show inline on the PR — the two
   352	flags compose (stdout stays pure JSON).

